def led (i = 10 mA)
  title "LED with series resistor, driven by a signal"
  about "A GPIO or logic output drives an LED to ground through a series resistor; the resistor sets the current, I =
      (V_in - Vf) / R."
  port in "the driving signal: a GPIO or a logic output"
  port gnd "ground"
  part R1 resistor 0805W8F1500T5E lcsc C17471 value 150 "{as}: LED series resistor"
    notes "150 Ω with this green LED (Vf 2.6 to 3.1 V): about 1.6 to 4 mA from a 3.3 V GPIO, 10 to 15 mA from 5 V – at
      the high end of Vf it still lights (SIM.state: at least 1 mA). Another current: another value of the same series
      (part-search skill)."
  part D1 led KT-0805G lcsc C2297 value green "{as}: LED (green 0805)"
  net in R1.1
  net anode @in R1.2 D1.anode
  net gnd D1.cathode
  derive R1.value series_resistor(i)
  covers LIM.v LIM.i LIM.p LIM.t LIM.range LIM.declared LIM.backfeed LVL.threshold LVL.direction SIM.config SIM.state
  notes "sim.json: the driving net under the MCU's outputs, and leds_on in every load – the LED's designator where it
      lights, [] or the others where it is dark (SIM.driver); edakit sim then checks the current against the LED and
      the MCU pin (LIM) and that it lights or stays dark (SIM.state)."
  notes "From an MCU pin keep the current within the pin's rating (gpio_i_max_a in the MCU's parts entry); for more
      light a transistor (block npn-switch) or an LED driver."
